/**
\defgroup distrib_truncated Truncated distribution
\brief Wrapper restricting a univariate distribution to a fixed window and renormalizing it there.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/truncated.hpp>` in namespace `laplace::distrib`, this module provides \ref laplace::distrib::truncated_distrib "truncated_distrib\<D\>" and the host factory \ref laplace::distrib::truncated "truncated()". Evaluation, derivative, cumulative, quantile, atom, and descriptor members of `truncated_distrib` are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution, while `start_value()` and `truncated()` run on the host only. See \ref distrib_core for the shared distribution interface, \ref distrib_atoms "the atom descriptors" for declared atoms, \ref distrib_generic for the generic routes, and \ref distrib_atom_mixture for the atom-mixture wrapper. In `tests/distrib/test_truncated.cpp`, internal consistency checks on the `gcc` and CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER) builds verify densities, sums, and integrals to \f$10^{-10}\f$, and cumulative inversion within \f$10^{-9}\f$. Narrow-window \f$\log Z\f$ and its derivatives to order \f$4\f$ match mpmath references within \f$4 \times 10^{-15}\f$ absolute and \f$5 \times 10^{-13}\f$ relative. The same suite checks parameter derivatives to order \f$6\f$ against five-point central differences within \f$2 \times 10^{-5}\f$ (density) and \f$3 \times 10^{-5}\f$ (cumulative tails) relative, and device evaluation against host within \f$10^{-11}\f$.

## Truncated law

With fixed bounds \f$a\f$ (`lower`) and \f$b\f$ (`upper`), both belonging to the window and held as values independent of the natural parameters \f$\theta\f$, the truncated law has log-density (or log-mass)
\f[
\ell(y; \theta) = \log f(y; \theta) - \log Z(\theta), \qquad
Z(\theta) = F(b; \theta) - F(a^-; \theta),
\f]
for \f$a \le y \le b\f$, and \f$-\infty\f$ (`-distrib_infinity`) outside. Here \f$y\f$ is the observation, \f$f\f$ is the parent's density or probability mass function, \f$F\f$ is the parent's cumulative distribution function, and \f$F(a^-)\f$ is its left limit at \f$a\f$, so that any point mass the parent puts at \f$a\f$ belongs to the window. For a discrete parent (`D::kind == distrib_kind::discrete`), whose support is the integers, `lower_end()` returns \f$\lceil a \rceil\f$ and `upper_end()` returns \f$\lfloor b \rfloor\f$, so the window consists of the integers from \f$\lceil a \rceil\f$ to \f$\lfloor b \rfloor\f$. For a continuous parent, `lower_end()` is \f$a\f$ and `upper_end()` is \f$b\f$.

The wrapper adds no parameter: `n_params`, `params`, and `kind` are the parent's, and `n_atoms` equals \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_n_atoms "distrib_n_atoms\<D\>()" (with `y_regularity` matching the parent for continuous parents). Because the bounds do not move with \f$\theta\f$, the normalization \f$Z(\theta)\f$ is differentiated under the integral sign and no parameter moves the support. The type `truncated_distrib<D>` is an aggregate with fields `parent`, `lower`, and `upper`, so it can be built directly inside a Kokkos kernel; the struct itself checks nothing. On the host, `truncated(parent, lower, upper)` validates the window and collapses nested truncations.

## Normalization

To prevent either cumulative value from being the complement of a small probability, \f$Z(\theta)\f$ is taken from whichever tail of the parent holds the smaller numbers. Writing \f$S(y; \theta) = 1 - F(y; \theta)\f$ and taking \f$b\f$ at `upper_end()`, `from_lower_tail()` selects between two tails:

- lower tail, used when \f$F(b; \theta) \le S(a^-; \theta)\f$: \f$Z(\theta) = F(b; \theta) - F(a^-; \theta)\f$;
- upper tail otherwise: \f$Z(\theta) = S(a^-; \theta) - S(b; \theta)\f$.

Infinite bounds \f$-\infty\f$ and \f$+\infty\f$ are read as limits \f$0\f$ and \f$1\f$ in the chosen tail. Either expression is a difference \f$Z = A - B\f$ of two numbers held on the log scale (\f$\log A\f$ and \f$\log B\f$), evaluated by `log_norm()` as
\f[
\log Z = \log A + \log\left(1 - e^{\log B - \log A}\right),
\f]
returning \f$-\infty\f$ when \f$\log B \ge \log A\f$ and \f$\log A\f$ when \f$\log B = -\infty\f$.

When \f$\log B - \log A > \log(15/16)\f$ (`detail_truncated::cancel_log`, that is \f$B > 15 A / 16\f$) and both endpoints are finite, the difference is not taken. Past that threshold, the subtraction loses more than four bits: the relative error in \f$Z\f$ is about \f$\varepsilon A / Z\f$, which grows like \f$\varepsilon / (b - a)\f$ on a narrow window, where \f$\varepsilon\f$ is double-precision machine epsilon. Instead, `log_norm()` sums the parent's mass over the interval on the log scale using log-sum-exp:

- for a continuous parent, a \f$16\f$-node Gauss-Legendre rule integrates the parent's density on each piece of the interval between the parent's `y_kinks()`, plus the mass of every atom the parent declares inside the interval;
- for a discrete parent, the parent's probability masses at the integers of the interval are summed whenever there are at most \f$64\f$ of them (`detail_truncated::max_summed_points`), and otherwise the difference is kept.

Because the density varies little on an interval short enough for \f$B > 15 A / 16\f$ to hold, \f$16\f$ quadrature nodes integrate it to machine precision. A density with a spike inside a window that is narrow relative to the parent's scale yet wide relative to the spike would need an adaptive rule.

Derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$ in \f$\theta\f$ to order \f$6\f$ (`log_norm_term<K>()`) come from the parent's own derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ or \f$\log S\f$ through the Bell relation of \ref distrib_generic read in the ratios. For a multiset \f$T\f$ of parameter indices,
\f[
\frac{\partial_T (A - B)}{A - B}
= \frac{A}{Z}\,\frac{\partial_T A}{A} - \frac{B}{Z}\,\frac{\partial_T B}{B},
\f]
and the derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$ follow from those ratios by the same Bell relation solved for them. When the mass is summed instead, the derivatives in \f$\theta\f$ to order \f$6\f$ come from the same sum, each term folded in through the same ratio relation with a plus sign in place of the minus sign. The parent's cumulative derivatives are read through the dispatchers \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_log_cdf_dk "distrib_log_cdf_dk()" and \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_log_ccdf_dk "distrib_log_ccdf_dk()" of \ref distrib_generic "the generic routes", so a parent without closed cumulative derivatives gets the generic ones.

## Left limit and atoms

By definition, \f$F(a^-) = F(a) - \mathbb{P}(Y = a)\f$. Both `left_limit_value()` and its derivative counterpart query the mass at \f$a\f$ from the parent object and never infer it from `D::kind`:

- For a continuous parent, \f$\mathbb{P}(Y = a)\f$ is the mass of an atom declared at \f$a\f$, read through \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_atom_point "distrib_atom_point()" and \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_atom_log_mass "distrib_atom_log_mass()" (\ref distrib_atoms "the atom descriptors"), and its parameter derivatives are the parent's log-density derivatives at \f$a\f$. With no atom at \f$a\f$, \f$F(a^-) = F(a)\f$ and \f$S(a^-) = S(a)\f$; on the upper tail with an atom, \f$S(a^-) = S(a) + \mathbb{P}(Y = a)\f$ is added on the log scale.
- For a discrete parent, \f$F(a^-)\f$ is evaluated directly as \f$F(\lceil a \rceil - 1)\f$ and \f$S(a^-)\f$ as \f$S(\lceil a \rceil - 1)\f$, which is the same value without performing a subtraction.

As an example, a zero-adjusted gamma distribution (\ref distrib_atom_mixture "the atom-mixture wrapper") truncated to \f$[0, 3]\f$ keeps its atom at \f$0\f$. Because the wrapper asks the parent and finds the atom at \f$0\f$, it evaluates \f$F(0^-) = 0\f$ and \f$Z = F(3)\f$. Reading the bound as a continuous point without querying the atom would instead give \f$Z = F(3) - \pi_0\f$, where \f$\pi_0\f$ is the atom mass. That would produce a law that does not integrate to one; `tests/distrib/test_truncated.cpp` checks that this truncated zero-adjusted gamma integrates to one with its atom.

## Member routes

### Log-density, log-mass, and derivatives

Member `log_value(y)` returns \f$\log f(y) - \log Z\f$ on `[lower_end(), upper_end()]` and \f$-\infty\f$ outside; `logpdf(y)` exposes it when `D::kind == distrib_kind::continuous`, and `logpmf(y)` exposes it when `D::kind == distrib_kind::discrete`. Parameter derivatives `log_value_dk<K>(y)` for order \f$1 \le K \le 6\f$ return the parent's tensor less that of \f$\log Z\f$ inside the window, and zero outside. They are exposed as `logpdf_dk<K>()`, `logpdf_d1()` through `logpdf_d6()`, `logpmf_dk<K>()`, and `logpmf_d1()` through `logpmf_d6()`.

For continuous parents, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` with observation order \f$J\f$ and parameter order \f$K\f$ (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) returns `parent.logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` when \f$J \ge 1\f$ because \f$\log Z\f$ does not depend on \f$y\f$. For \f$J = 0\f$, it returns `log_value(y)` in a `sym_tensor<n_params, 0>` when \f$K = 0\f$, and `log_value_dk<K>(y)` when \f$K \ge 1\f$. Calling `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` with \f$J \ge 1\f$ on a parent without that member is a compile-time error.

### Cumulative functions and quantiles

Let \f$F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y)\f$ denote the cumulative distribution function of the truncated law. Below `lower_end()`, `log_cdf_tail(y, upper_tail)` gives \f$F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) = 0\f$ (`log_cdf` equal to \f$-\infty\f$ and `log_ccdf` equal to \f$0\f$); from `upper_end()` onward, it gives \f$F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) = 1\f$ (`log_cdf` equal to \f$0\f$ and `log_ccdf` equal to \f$-\infty\f$). Inside `[lower_end(), upper_end())`, `log_cdf_tail()` (wrapped by `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`, `cdf()`, and `ccdf()`) evaluates each side as a difference of two of the parent's cumulative values over \f$Z\f$, taken on the tail that \f$Z\f$ is taken on:

- on the lower tail: \f$F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) = (F(y) - F(a^-)) / Z\f$ and \f$1 - F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) = (F(b) - F(y)) / Z\f$;
- on the upper tail: \f$F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) = (S(a^-) - S(y)) / Z\f$ and \f$1 - F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) = (S(y) - S(b)) / Z\f$.

Here \f$b\f$ is `upper_end()`. The same cancellation rule applies to each numerator: \f$F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y)\f$ uses the parent's mass on \f$[a, y]\f$ (closed at \f$a\f$) and \f$1 - F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y)\f$ uses the mass on \f$(y, b]\f$ (open at \f$y\f$), each summed instead of subtracted when its own two cumulative values satisfy \f$\log B - \log A > \log(15/16)\f$ and the interval is summable. Parameter derivatives `log_cdf_tail_dk<K>(y, upper_tail)` for \f$1 \le K \le 6\f$ (`log_cdf_d1()` through `log_cdf_d6()` and `log_ccdf_d1()` through `log_ccdf_d6()`) differentiate that numerator—via the two-term difference or the log-scale sum—through the same ratios and subtract the derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$. Outside `[lower_end(), upper_end())` or when the log-difference is \f$-\infty\f$, they return zero.

For probability \f$p \in (0, 1)\f$, `quantile(p)` evaluates \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile "distrib_quantile()" at \f$F(a^-) + p Z\f$ (computed on the log scale) when `from_lower_tail()` holds. On the upper tail, it forms \f$\log(S(b) + (1 - p) Z)\f$ and calls \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile_from_log_upper "distrib_quantile_from_log_upper()" for every parent, with or without atoms. The log-scale route serves a parent with atoms too, because the generic quantile inverts a cumulative function with jumps and returns the atom point when the target lies inside an atom's jump. The returned value is kept inside `[support_lower(), support_upper()]`; at \f$p = 0\f$ and \f$p = 1\f$ it returns `support_lower()` and `support_upper()`, and outside \f$[0, 1]\f$ or on `NaN` it returns quiet `NaN`.

When the window is summed, as described under Normalization, the target \f$F(a^-) + p Z\f$ (or \f$\log(S(b) + (1 - p) Z)\f$) is itself a difference that cancels. The quantile then misses by about \f$\varepsilon F(a) / f(y)\f$, where \f$\varepsilon\f$ is the double-precision machine epsilon, \f$f\f$ the parent's density and \f$w = b - a\f$ the width of the window. That is the fraction \f$\varepsilon F(a) / Z\f$ of \f$w\f$. Two repairs follow.

For a continuous parent, after the parent's quantile is kept inside the support, one Newton step is taken on the summed mass. Let \f$M(a, y)\f$ be the parent's mass on \f$[a, y]\f$, summed as described under Normalization, and \f$M(y, b)\f$ the summed mass on \f$(y, b]\f$. For \f$p \le 1/2\f$ the residual is \f$r = M(a, y) / Z - p\f$; for \f$p > 1/2\f$ it is \f$r = (1 - p) - M(y, b) / Z\f$. Then \f$y \leftarrow y - r / f_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y)\f$, where \f$f_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) = f(y) / Z\f$ is the truncated density, and the result is again kept inside the support. The step is skipped when \f$y\f$ is an atom point of the parent, where the quantile is the atom.

For a discrete parent, the quantile is the first integer \f$k\f$ of the window whose summed mass from the lower bound, the sum of the masses of the integers from \f$\lceil a \rceil\f$ to \f$k\f$, reaches \f$pZ\f$ (compared on the log scale). It is \f$\lfloor b \rfloor\f$ when no integer before it does.

### Expected information, atoms, support, and starting values

The wrapper declares no `expected_info()` or `expected_info_dk()` members, because the expected Fisher information of a truncated law is not the parent's; the generic route of \ref distrib_generic answers by integrating over the window. When `distrib_n_atoms<D>() > 0`, `atom_point(i)` returns `distrib_atom_point(parent, i)` and `atom_log_mass(i)` returns `distrib_atom_log_mass(parent, i) - log_norm()` for atom index \f$i\f$.

Support bounds `support_lower()` and `support_upper()` cut the parent's support to `[lower_end(), upper_end()]`. Member `center()` moves `parent.center()` into `[support_lower(), support_upper()]`, and `scale()` returns the parent's scale or `support_upper() - support_lower()` when that width is smaller. Member `y_kinks()` (continuous parents) returns `parent.y_kinks()`, and `param_kinks(j, y)` returns `parent.param_kinks(j, y)` for parameter index \f$j\f$. On the host, `start_value(const std::vector<double>& y)` passes the whole sample `y` to \ref distrib_start "distrib_start_value(parent, y)" and returns a `truncated_distrib` with the same `lower` and `upper`. The start therefore estimates the untruncated law, not the truncated one. On samples of \f$200\f$ and \f$5000\f$ points drawn from Gaussians truncated to five windows and from gamma2 laws truncated to two, its scale is about half the truncated maximum-likelihood estimate's, and its location is pulled into the window. A damped Newton iteration from this start reached the estimate in \f$9\f$ to \f$21\f$ steps in \f$13\f$ of the \f$14\f$ cases. In the remaining case, on \f$[0, 0.5]\f$ with \f$200\f$ points, the likelihood is flat in the scale and the iteration stopped elsewhere.

## Factory, refusals, and collapse

The host factory \ref laplace::distrib::truncated "truncated(parent, lower, upper)" first cuts `[lower, upper]` to the parent's support `[parent.support_lower(), parent.support_upper()]`. When `parent` is already a `truncated_distrib`, `truncated()` collapses the nested truncation into a single `truncated_distrib` over the innermost parent at the intersection of the two windows. Writing the nested type `truncated_distrib<truncated_distrib<D>>` directly is refused at compile time by a `static_assert` telling the caller to use `truncated()`.

After cutting the window to the parent's support, `truncated()` throws `std::invalid_argument` in three situations:

- the rounded window holds no point or a single point (`!(t.lower_end() < t.upper_end())`), where no parameter is identified;
- the window leaves out an atom of the parent, so the coordinate that weighs it would have an identically zero score; the message names the equivalent model, namely the atom mixture over the atoms inside the window truncated to it, or the parent alone truncated when no atom remains (for an `atom_mixture_distrib` over a discrete parent, whose atoms are support points and are not declared as atoms, the points it weighs are checked the same way);
- the window holds no mass of the parent (`!(t.log_norm() > -distrib_infinity)`).

A window that keeps every atom of the parent is accepted. Building an \ref distrib_atom_mixture "atom_mixture_distrib" over a `truncated_distrib` parent is also accepted whether its atoms lie inside or outside the truncation window.

## Checks

`tests/distrib/test_truncated.cpp` runs \f$17\f$ test cases and \f$4048\f$ assertions on the `gcc` build and the same counts on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER). Narrow-window values are checked against one reference file, `tests/data/reference/distrib_truncated_narrow.csv`, written by `tools/reference/distrib_truncated_narrow.py` with mpmath (The mpmath development team, 2023); every other quantity has a twin inside the test. The reference file holds \f$\log Z\f$ of `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` on \f$[a, b]\f$ for \f$a \in \{1, 4, -3\}\f$ and \f$w \in \{10^{-2}, 10^{-4}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-8}, 10^{-10}, 10^{-12}\}\f$, together with its derivatives in \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ to order \f$4\f$, where the upper bound \f$b\f$ is the `double` nearest to \f$a + w\f$, stored as it is. Its values come from `mpmath.diff` of the definition \f$\log(\Phi((b - \mu)/\sigma) - \Phi((a - \mu)/\sigma))\f$ at \f$400\f$ and \f$500\f$ bits, keeping a row when the two agree to \f$2^{-80}\f$ relative (\f$270\f$ rows, none dropped).

| Check in `tests/distrib/test_truncated.cpp` | Scope | Test tolerance |
|---|---|---|
| `log_norm()` vs `erfc` difference | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` on middle, narrow (\f$[1, 1.01]\f$), deep-tail (\f$[10, 12]\f$, \f$[-12, -10]\f$), and half-line windows | \f$10^{-12}\f$ |
| `logpdf()` vs parent less `log_norm()` | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` across all six windows above (outside by \f$10^{-9}\f$ gives \f$-\infty\f$) | \f$10^{-12}\f$ |
| `panels_quad()` integral of density | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` across all six windows above | \f$10^{-10}\f$ |
| Discrete masses, normalization, and CDF sum | `poisson(3.5)` on \f$[2, 9]\f$ and \f$[1.5, 9.7]\f$; `poisson(0.3)` on \f$[1, \infty)\f$ | \f$10^{-15}\f$ to \f$10^{-13}\f$ |
| `log_norm()` and `log_norm_term<4>()` vs mpmath | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` on \f$[a, b]\f$ for \f$a \in \{1, 4, -3\}\f$ and \f$w \in \{10^{-2}, \dots, 10^{-12}\}\f$ (\f$270\f$ rows) | \f$4 \times 10^{-15}\f$ absolute on \f$\log Z\f$ (measured \f$2.03 \times 10^{-15}\f$); \f$5 \times 10^{-13}\f$ on derivatives relative to largest reference of same order and window (measured \f$2.28 \times 10^{-13}\f$) |
| Summed Poisson masses | `poisson(1000)` truncated to \f$[1000, 1001]\f$ vs direct sum of the two masses; derivatives vs five-point differences | \f$4 \times 10^{-15}\f$ (measured \f$0\f$) on \f$\log Z\f$; \f$2 \times 10^{-5}\f$ on derivatives |
| `logpdf_dk<1..6>()` and `logpmf_dk<1..6>()` vs five-point differences | Middle, near bounds, narrow window, both tails, half-line, and discrete bounds, relative to order scale | \f$2 \times 10^{-5}\f$ |
| `cdf()` and `cdf() + ccdf() = 1` vs `panels_quad()` | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` on middle, narrow, and both tail windows (`cdf` \f$10^{-10}\f$, sum \f$10^{-13}\f$) | \f$10^{-10}\f$ to \f$10^{-13}\f$ |
| `log_cdf_dk<1..6>()` and `log_ccdf_dk<1..6>()` vs five-point differences | `gaussian1` and `poisson`, both tails, relative to order scale | \f$3 \times 10^{-5}\f$ |
| `quantile(p)` inverts `cdf()` | `gaussian1` on middle and both tail windows; `poisson` exact integer inequality; narrow windows \f$[a, a + w]\f$ with \f$a \in \{0, -10^{-3}, 1, 4, -3\}\f$ and \f$w \in \{10^{-2}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-12}\}\f$ at five values of \f$p\f$; `poisson(1000)` on \f$[1000, 1001]\f$; upper tail of the two-atom `gaussian1` on \f$[0, 20]\f$ at \f$1 - p = 10^{-5}, 10^{-10}, 10^{-15}\f$ | \f$10^{-9}\f$; narrow windows \f$\lvert F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y) - p \rvert \le 10^{-13} + 8 \times 10^{-16} \lvert y \rvert / w\f$, where \f$w\f$ is the width of the window and the second term is of the order of the ulp of \f$y\f$ over \f$w\f$, below which no double can go; upper tail \f$1 - F_{\mathrm{trunc}}(y)\f$ against \f$1 - p\f$ to \f$10^{-12}\f$ |
| Atom-carrying truncated laws (`panels_quad()` plus atoms, derivatives, quantile) | Zero-adjusted `gamma2` on \f$[0, 3]\f$, two-atom `gaussian1` on \f$[0, 2]\f$, and atom at \f$0\f$ over `gaussian1` on \f$[0.5, 3]\f$ | \f$10^{-10}\f$ for the mass, \f$2 \times 10^{-5}\f$ for the log-density derivatives and \f$3 \times 10^{-5}\f$ for the cumulative ones |
| Atom on the upper bound | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` with an adjusted atom at \f$2\f$ (`atom_alr` \f$-1\f$) truncated to \f$[0, 2]\f$, and `poisson(3.5)` inflated at \f$9\f$ and truncated to \f$[2, 9]\f$. Checked: the total mass; \f$\log Z\f$ from the lower tail \f$F(2) - F(0)\f$ and from the upper tail \f$S(0) - S(2)\f$, which both match the mass, since \f$F(b)\f$ holds the atom's jump and \f$S(b)\f$ does not; \f$\mathrm{cdf}(b) = 1\f$ and \f$\mathrm{ccdf}(b) = 0\f$; \f$1 - F_{\mathrm{trunc}}\f$ just below the bound, at \f$2 - 10^{-3}\f$ and \f$2 - 10^{-9}\f$, against the atom's mass plus the continuous mass in closed form; the quantile at \f$p\f$ inside the jump; derivatives against five-point differences; the window \f$[2 - 10^{-8}, 2]\f$ | mass \f$10^{-10}\f$ (Poisson \f$10^{-14}\f$); \f$\log Z\f$ from both tails \f$10^{-14}\f$; \f$\mathrm{cdf}(b)\f$ and \f$\mathrm{ccdf}(b)\f$ exact; \f$1 - F_{\mathrm{trunc}}\f$ near the bound \f$10^{-12}\f$; quantile inverts the cdf below the jump \f$10^{-9}\f$; derivatives \f$2 \times 10^{-5}\f$ (log-density) and \f$3 \times 10^{-5}\f$ (tails); window \f$\log Z\f$ \f$10^{-14}\f$ |
| Generic `distrib_expected_info<0>()` and `distrib_expected_info<1>()` | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` on \f$[-0.5, 2.0]\f$ vs `panels_quad()` of \f$-\mathbb{E}[\partial^2\ell / \partial\theta_j \partial\theta_k]\f$ (\f$10^{-9}\f$) and differences (\f$10^{-6}\f$) | \f$10^{-9}\f$ (`K = 0`), \f$10^{-6}\f$ (`K = 1`) |
| `logpdf_dy_dk<1, 2>()` and `logpdf_dy_dk<0, 3>()` | `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` on \f$[-0.5, 2.0]\f$ vs parent and `logpdf_dk<3>()` | Exact |
| Kokkos device kernel vs host evaluation | Truncated `gaussian1` on \f$[-0.5, 3]\f$ and on \f$[1, 1 + 10^{-8}]\f$, with the quantile in both windows; zero-adjusted `gamma2` and `poisson` densities, derivatives and tails | \f$10^{-11}\f$ |

The table below reports the largest absolute error of \f$\log Z\f$ across the three windows of each width \f$w\f$ against the mpmath references, which equals the relative error of \f$Z\f$, comparing the cumulative-value difference used when \f$B \le 15 A / 16\f$ with the summed mass:

| \f$w\f$ | difference of cumulative values | summed mass |
|---|---|---|
| \f$10^{-2}\f$ | \f$1.4 \times 10^{-14}\f$ to \f$3.9 \times 10^{-14}\f$ | at most \f$9 \times 10^{-16}\f$ |
| \f$10^{-4}\f$ | \f$2.6 \times 10^{-13}\f$ to \f$4.7 \times 10^{-12}\f$ | at most \f$1.8 \times 10^{-15}\f$ |
| \f$10^{-6}\f$ | \f$2.2 \times 10^{-10}\f$ to \f$2.5 \times 10^{-10}\f$ | \f$0\f$ |
| \f$10^{-8}\f$ | \f$8.6 \times 10^{-9}\f$ to \f$4.2 \times 10^{-8}\f$ | \f$0\f$ |
| \f$10^{-10}\f$ | \f$1.6 \times 10^{-6}\f$ to \f$3.9 \times 10^{-6}\f$ | \f$0\f$ |
| \f$10^{-12}\f$ | \f$2.5 \times 10^{-4}\f$ to \f$4.3 \times 10^{-4}\f$ | \f$0\f$ |

The difference of the cumulative values loses about \f$4 \times 10^{-16} / w\f$, whereas the summed mass keeps the machine precision.

The quantile is measured against mpmath at \f$300\f$ bits, by bisection on the definition of the cumulative function. The law is `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` truncated to \f$[a, a + w]\f$, with \f$w\f$ from \f$10^{-2}\f$ to \f$10^{-12}\f$ and \f$p \in \{10^{-6}, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 1 - 10^{-6}\}\f$. The table gives the largest error over these cases, in units in the last place of \f$y\f$ (see \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured"), for the parent's quantile alone and with the Newton step described in the section "Cumulative functions and quantiles".

| \f$a\f$ | Parent quantile alone | With the Newton step |
|---|---|---|
| \f$1, 4, -3, 0.4\f$ | \f$0.1\f$ to \f$12\f$ ulps | \f$0.1\f$ to \f$0.5\f$ ulps |
| \f$-10^{-3}\f$ | \f$1.3 \times 10^{3}\f$ to \f$3.1 \times 10^{5}\f$ ulps | \f$0.4\f$ to \f$0.5\f$ ulps; \f$1.9 \times 10^{3}\f$ ulps at \f$w = 10^{-2}\f$ |
| \f$0\f$ | \f$5.4 \times 10^{7}\f$ to \f$8.1 \times 10^{15}\f$ ulps | \f$3.9\f$ to \f$24\f$ ulps |

Where \f$\lvert y \rvert\f$ is of the order of \f$F(a) / f(a)\f$, the error of the parent's quantile alone was already close to the ulp of \f$y\f$. Near zero it was not: at \f$a = 0\f$ and \f$w = 10^{-12}\f$ the parent's quantile missed by \f$2 \times 10^{-4}\f$ of the window. With the step, the remaining error at \f$a = 0\f$ is at most \f$1.6 \times 10^{-15}\f$ of the window, and the \f$1.9 \times 10^{3}\f$ ulps at \f$a = -10^{-3}\f$, \f$w = 10^{-2}\f$ are \f$1.3 \times 10^{-16}\f$ of the window, at a quantile close to zero whose ulp is far smaller than the bounds'. A second Newton step was measured and gave no better result.

The log-scale upper-tail route is checked with atoms as well. The law is `gaussian1(0.4, 1.3)` with adjusted atoms at \f$0\f$ and \f$1\f$ (`atom_alr` \f$-1\f$ and \f$-2\f$), truncated to \f$[0, 8]\f$ and to \f$[0, 20]\f$, at \f$p = 1 - 10^{-k}\f$ for \f$k = 1\f$ to \f$16\f$. Over these points the quantile agrees with mpmath to a relative error of at most \f$1.9 \times 10^{-16}\f$.

## Kokkos usage

The following kernel builds a truncated Gaussian distribution on the host and evaluates its log-density, score, and cumulative distribution in parallel:

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/gaussian1.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/truncated.hpp>

void eval_truncated_gaussian(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out,
                             double mu, double sigma, double lower, double upper) {
  const auto d = laplace::distrib::truncated(laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib{mu, sigma}, lower, upper);
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_trunc_gau", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpdf(y(i)) + d.logpdf_d1(y(i))[0] + d.cdf(y(i));
  });
}
```

## Members

Namespace `laplace::distrib` provides:

- \ref laplace::distrib::truncated_distrib "truncated_distrib\<D\>" — distribution wrapper with fields `parent`, `lower`, and `upper`, static descriptors `kind`, `n_params`, `params`, `n_atoms`, and `y_regularity` (continuous parents), boundary and normalization helpers `lower_end()`, `upper_end()`, `left_limit_value()`, `from_lower_tail()`, `log_norm()`, and `log_norm_term()`, density and derivative evaluators `log_value()`, `log_value_dk()`, `logpdf()`, `logpdf_d1()`..`logpdf_d6()`, `logpdf_dk()`, `logpmf()`, `logpmf_d1()`..`logpmf_d6()`, `logpmf_dk()`, and `logpdf_dy_dk()`, cumulative and quantile members `log_cdf_tail()`, `log_cdf_tail_dk()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`, `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf_d1()`..`log_cdf_d6()`, `log_ccdf_d1()`..`log_ccdf_d6()`, and `quantile()`, atom queries `atom_point()` and `atom_log_mass()`, domain and kink queries `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `y_kinks()`, and `param_kinks()`, and host starting value `start_value()`.
- \ref laplace::distrib::truncated "truncated()" — host factory that intersects the window with the parent's support, collapses nested truncations, and checks that the window holds more than one point, keeps all weighed atom points, and carries positive parent mass.

## References

- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
*/
